"Hyperbolic type distances in starlike domains (https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.00590)\nciteturn6academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.7 years ago; We study the growth of hyperbolic type distances in starlike domains.\nTitle: Hyperbolic type distances in starlike domains\nAuthors: Riku Klén\nDate: Wed Mar  2 06:29:24 2016\n\nWe study the growth of hyperbolic type distances in starlike domains. We derive estimates for various hyperbolic type distances and consider the asymptotic sharpness of the estimates.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHyperbolic Type Distances in Starlike Domains | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/301841395_Hyperbolic_Type_Distances_in_Starlike_Domains)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; We study the growth of hyperbolic type distances in starlike domains. ... Hyperbolic type distance, starlike domain. ... similar applications to geometric function theory in higher dimensions [14,15,\n\n# Hyperbolic Type Distances in Starlike Domains\n\n  * September 2017\n  * Results in Mathematics 72(1)\n\nDOI:10.1007/s00025-016-0642-8\n\nAuthors:\n\nRiku Klén\n\n  * University of Turku\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRead publisher previewRequest full-text PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research, you can request a copy directly from the author.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study the growth of hyperbolic type distances in starlike domains. We derive estimates for various hyperbolic type distances and consider the asymptotic sharpness of the estimates.\n\n\nResults Math 72 (2017), 47–69\n\nc\n\n2017 Springer International Publishing\n\n1422-6383/17/010047-23\n\npublished online January 6, 2017\n\nDOI 10.1007/s00025-016-0642-8 Results in Mathematics\n\nHyperbolic Type Distances in Starlike\n\nDomains\n\nRiku Kl´en\n\nAbstract. We study the growth of hyperbolic type distances in starlike\n\ndomains. We derive estimates for various hyperbolic type distances and\n\nconsider the asymptotic sharpness of the estimates.\n\nMathematics Subject Classiﬁcation. 30F45, 51M10, 30C65.\n\nKeywords. Hyperbolic type distance, starlike domain.\n\n1. Introduction\n\nThe hyperbolic distance has turned out to be a useful tool in geometric func-\n\ntion theory. The basic models for the hyperbolic distance are the unit ball\n\nmodel and the upper half space model. Using these models in the plane case\n\nn= 2, we can ﬁnd the hyperbolic distance in any simply connected domain\n\nwith at least 2 boundary points via the Riemann mapping theorem. In higher\n\ndimensions n≥3, there are no such results we could use to consider the hyper-\n\nbolic distance in general domains. A solution to this is to use other distance\n\nfunctions, which approximate the hyperbolic distance and are easier to eval-\n\nuate. We call a distance function hyperbolic type, if it is comparable to the\n\nhyperbolic distance.\n\nThe hyperbolic distance have had many applications to the geometric\n\nfunction theory in the complex plain [4]. The hyperbolic type metrics have\n\nsimilar applications to geometric function theory in higher dimensions [14,15,\n\n21].--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHyperbolic type distances in starlike domains | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/386716176_Hyperbolic_type_distances_in_starlike_domains)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.7 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.1603.00590 ... We study the growth of hyperbolic type distances in starlike domains. ... We find sharp bounds for $v_G$ in terms of the hyperbolic metric in the particular case when the domain is either the unit ball ${\\mathbb {B}}^n$ or the upper half space ${\\mathbb {H}}^n$ . ... The quasihyperbolic metric has been found to have many interesting and varied applications in geometric function theory.\n\nPreprint\n\n# Hyperbolic type distances in starlike domains\n\n  * March 2016\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.1603.00590\n\nAuthors:\n\nRiku Klén\n\n  * University of Turku\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study the growth of hyperbolic type distances in starlike domains. We derive estimates for various hyperbolic type distances and consider the asymptotic sharpness of the estimates.\n\n\n## No file available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the author.\n\nRequest file PDF\n\n## Citations (0)\n\n## References (15)\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any citations for this publication.\n\nThe Visual Angle Metric and Möbius Transformations\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Oct 2014\n\n  * Riku Klén\n  * Henri Lindén\n  * Matti Vuorinen\n  * Gendi Wang\n\nA new similarity invariant metric $v_G$ is introduced. The visual angle metric $v_G$ is defined on a domain $G\\subsetneq {{\\mathbb {R}}}^n$ whose boundary is not a proper subset of a line. We find sharp bounds for $v_G$ in terms of the hyperbolic metric in the particular case when the domain is either the unit ball ${\\mathbb {B}}^n$ or the upper half space ${\\mathbb {H}}^n$ . We also obtain the sharp Lipschitz constant for a Möbius transformation $f: G\\rightarrow G'$ between domains G and $G'$ in ${{\\mathbb {R}}}^n$ with respect to the metrics $v_G$ and $v_{G'}$ . For instance, in the case $G=G'={\\mathbb {B}}^n$ the result is sharp.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nSharp Distortion Theorems for Quasiconformal Mappings\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Jan 1988\n\n  * Glen D Anderson\n  * M.K. Vamanamurthy\n  * Matti Vuorinen\n\nContinuing their earlier work on distortion theory, the authors prove some dimension-free distortion theorems for K K -quasiconformal mappings in R n {R^n} . For example, one of the present results is the following sharp variant of the Schwarz lemma: If f f is a K K -quasiconformal self-mapping of the unit ball B n {B^n} , n ⩾ 2 n \\geqslant 2 , with f ( 0 ) = 0 f(0) = 0 , then 4 1 − K 2 | x | K ⩽ | f ( x ) | ⩽ 4 1 − 1 / K 2 | x | 1 / K {4^{1 - {K^2}}}|x{|^K} \\leqslant |f(x)| \\leqslant {4^{1 - 1/{K^2}}}|x{|^{1/K}} for all x x in B n {B^n} .\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nConformal invariants and quasiregular mappings\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Dec 1985\n\n  * Matti Vuorinen\n\nView\n\nUniform domains and the quasi-hyperbolic metric\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Dec 1979\n\n  * F. W. Gehring\n  * Brad Osgood\n\nView\n\nThe quasihyperbolic metric and associated estimates on the hyperbolic metric\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Dec 1986\n\n  * Gaven John Martin\n  * Brad Osgood\n\nIn this paper we investigate the geometry of the quasihyperbolic metric of domains in R\". This metric arises from the conformally flat generalized Riemannian metric d(x, aD)-'ldx I. Due to the fact that the density cl(x, aD) -t is not necessarily differentiable, the classical theories of Riemannian geometry do not apply to this metric. The quasihyperbolic metric has been found to have many interesting and varied applications in geometric function theory. In particular, quasiconformal mappings are quasi-isometries of this metric for sufficiently far-lying points, also bounds on the quasihyperbolic metric in terms of other metrics imply that a domain is uniform which then implies certain injectivity criteria for locally-Lipschitz mappings, amongst others. In fact there is quite a strong relationship between uniform domains and the quasihyperbolic metric. Most of these basic results on the quasihyperbolic metric can be found in (3), (2) and (5). We note here that the quasihyperbolic metric is complete and generates the usual topology on a proper subdomain of R\". Further, geodesics (length minimizing curves) always exist for this metric and these geodesics have Lipschitz continuous first derivatives, which is in fact best possible. We begin by calculating the quasihyperbolic metric, its curvature and geodesics for some nontrivial examples in R'. We also calculate the possible isometries and show that they are conformal mappings, and so when n > 2 are M6bius transfor- mations. We then turn to the planar case for a more detailed investigation. Here it becomes possible to compute the Gaussian curvature of the quasihyperbolic metric in some basic examples and use these for comparison in more general cases. In particular, we show that the quasihyperbolic metric of a planar domain is an S-K metric, in the sense of Heins, if and only if the domain is convex. We denote euclidean n-space by R\" and its one point compactification by R'.\n\nView\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Klen_R in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AKlen_R+in%3Amath)\nciteturn6search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; @misc{1603.00590, author = {Riku Klén}, title = {{H}yperbolic type distances in starlike domains}, year = {2016}, eprint = {1603.00590}, note = {arXiv:1603.00590v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... We study the growth of hyperbolic type distances in starlike domains. ... Our main result is that in uniformly smooth Banach spaces a quasihyperbolic ball of a convex domain is $C^1$-smooth.\n\n32 results for au:Klen_R in:math\n\nShow all abstracts\n\n  * Ptolemy constant and uniformity\n\nEero Harmaala, Riku Klén\n\nApr 20 2016 math.MG arXiv:1604.05367v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{1604.05367, author = {Eero Harmaala and Riku Klén}, title = {{P}tolemy constant and uniformity}, year = {2016}, eprint = {1604.05367}, note = {arXiv:1604.05367v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe study Ptolemy constant and uniformity constant in various plane domains including triangles, quadrilaterals and ellipses.\n\n  * Hyperbolic type distances in starlike domains\n\nRiku Klén\n\nMar 03 2016 math.MG arXiv:1603.00590v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{1603.00590, author = {Riku Klén}, title = {{H}yperbolic type d